          'A chord keeps its identity however its notes are distributed; what changes with the bass note is the inversion. Root position puts the root in the bass; first inversion the third; second inversion the fifth; and for seventh chords, third inversion puts the seventh itself in the bass. To find the root of any voicing, stack the pitches into the tightest pile of thirds — the bottom of that stack is the root. Inversions are not decoration: a first-inversion triad lightens the harmony and smooths the bass line, while the second inversion is so unstable that tonal style confines it to a few formulaic uses you will meet in week 11.',
          'Figured bass, the Baroque shorthand, numbers the intervals sounding above the bass note. Root-position triads take 5/3, abbreviated to nothing; first inversion is 6 (short for 6/3); second inversion 6/4. Seventh chords run 7, 6/5, 4/3 and 4/2 for root position through third inversion — memorize the descending pattern 7, 65, 43, 42. An accidental standing alone applies to the third above the bass, and a slash or plus through a figure raises that pitch. These symbols merge with Roman numerals into the analytical dialect of this course (V6/5, ii6), and they were once performance notation: a continuo harpsichordist in a Corelli sonata read exactly these figures and improvised the harmony in real time.',
