          'Predominant chords occupy the syntactic slot between tonic and dominant, and the two diatonic staples are IV and ii. Both contain scale degrees 4 and 6, the notes that most smoothly approach the dominant’s 5 and 7; ii has the added kinetic advantage of standing a fifth above V, so ii–V participates in the fundamental root motion of tonal music, the descending fifth. In practice ii appears most often in first inversion, ii6, which puts scale degree 4 in the bass — giving the bass the same note as IV while the upper voices gain the fifth-motion energy of ii, a trade so favorable that ii6 is arguably the most common predominant in the Classical style. In minor, watch the qualities: iio is diminished and appears almost exclusively as iio6, while iv is minor.',
