          'Cadences are the punctuation marks of tonal syntax, classified by their final chords and their soprano. The perfect authentic cadence (PAC) — root-position V(7) to root-position I with the soprano ending on scale degree 1 — is the full stop, required for the strongest closes. The imperfect authentic cadence (IAC) weakens one condition (soprano ends on 3 or 5, or an inversion is involved) and reads as a lighter period. The half cadence (HC) stops on V itself, an open question, and it is how nearly every antecedent phrase ends; the deceptive cadence (V–vi) evades an expected resolution — famously and repeatedly in the final pages of Wagner’s Tristan — and the plagal IV–I, the Amen of hymnody, functions as a post-cadential benediction rather than true closure.',
          'The cadential six-four is the classic ornament of the authentic cadence and the most misread chord in beginning analysis. On the surface it spells a tonic triad in second inversion, but it functions as a decorated dominant: over a bass scale degree 5, the sixth and fourth above the bass are accented dissonances that resolve down by step to the fifth and third, turning the six-four into V(7). Hence the analytical notation V6/4–5/3, or cad.6/4, rather than a misleading I6/4. Its grammar is strict: bass note doubled, placed on a metrically stronger beat than its resolution, upper voices descending stepwise into the dominant.',
