          'Non-chord tones (NCTs) are pitches foreign to the sounding harmony, and they carry most of tonal music’s melodic expressivity. Each type is defined by approach and resolution: the passing tone fills a third by step in one direction; the neighbor tone steps away and back; the appoggiatura arrives by leap and resolves by step (usually down), landing accented and aching; the escape tone steps away and leaps off; the anticipation arrives early at the next chord’s pitch. The accented–unaccented distinction matters as much as the type: an accented passing tone bites where an unaccented one glides.',
          'The suspension deserves its own paragraph because it is the queen of dissonances and the most rule-bound. It unfolds in three phases: preparation (the note sounds as a consonance), suspension (the harmony changes beneath it, making it dissonant on the strong beat), and resolution (down by step to a chord tone). Suspensions are named by the intervals above the bass: 4–3, 7–6, 9–8, and in the bass itself 2–3; the retardation is the same device resolving upward, classically at final cadences (7–8 against the tonic). Chains of suspensions power whole passages of Corelli and Bach, and the crunching 9–8 and 4–3 chains in Pergolesi’s Stabat Mater show how pure voice-leading formula becomes grief made audible.',
