          'The keyboard is the instrument on which Western theory became visible, and that is why every conservatory requires it of every student. Its layout encodes the diatonic system: the irregular pattern of two- and three-black-key groups exists so that the seven white keys spell a major scale from C, and so that the half steps of that scale — E–F and B–C — appear as the places where no black key intervenes. Learning to see the keyboard as a repeating twelve-semitone module rather than a row of undifferentiated keys is the first task, and it is a visual skill: name pitches by their position relative to the black-key groups, not by counting upward from C.',
